    "bank": "gradegain",
    "module": "neuropsych",
    "chapter": "nr-cranial",
    "stem": "A 45-year-old female presents with a complaint of a superficial, electric-like sensation on the right side of her face, lasting for a few seconds. The sensation is sudden and sharp, occurring intermittently, and is localized around the cheek and jaw. She denies any facial weakness or numbness. The patient reports that the symptoms seem to be triggered by light touch, such as brushing her face or chewing. There is no associated vision or hearing problem. Neurological examination is normal, except for tenderness over the right maxillary area. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "options": [
      "Facial Nerve Affection",
      "Trigeminal Neuralgia",
      "Stroke",
      "Myofascial spasm"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explanation": "**Trigeminal neuralgia. Cheek and jaw is V2 and V3 territory, the pain is electric and lasts seconds, light touch triggers it, and the examination is otherwise normal — every one of those is a defining feature.**\n\n`L8) Cranial nerves` defines the syndrome in a single sentence that this vignette follows almost word for word:\n\n> “TN is defined clinically by **paroxysmal, stereotyped attacks of usually intense, sharp, superficial or stabbing pain, unilateral** in the distribution of one or more branches of the fifth cranial (trigeminal) nerve.”\n\n**Three details in the stem are doing specific work:**\n\n1. **“Cheek and jaw”** is the classic distribution. `L8`: “The distribution of pain most often involves the **V2 and/or V3** subdivisions of the trigeminal nerve” — maxillary and mandibular. This is the commonest presentation, not an unusual one.\n2. **“Triggered by light touch, such as brushing her face or chewing”** names the trigger zone. `L8`: “**Trigger zones** in the distribution of the affected nerve. Triggers of TN paroxysms include **lightly touching of this zones, chewing, talking, brushing teeth, cold air, smiling, and/or grimacing**.” It is the paradoxical quality that clinches it — a light touch fires the pain while firm pressure often does not.\n3. **“Denies any facial weakness or numbness” with a normal examination** is what excludes the alternatives. TN is a pure pain disorder: it has no motor sign and no sensory loss. **A patient with facial numbness or weakness alongside the pain must be investigated for a structural lesion**, since `L8` reserves the label “secondary TN” for cases “caused by structural lesions… e.g., multiple sclerosis, post-herpetic, and tumor.”\n\n**Her age and sex fit too:** “TN is one of the most frequently seen neuralgias in the **elderly**. It usually starts between the ages of **50 and 60**. **Women** tend to be affected more than men.” At 45 she is a little young, but well within range.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Facial nerve affection** | the facial nerve (VII) is **motor** to the muscles of expression. Its lesion causes **weakness**, which the stem explicitly denies. Facial *sensation* is trigeminal, not facial-nerve |\n| **Stroke** | sudden, but produces a **deficit** — weakness, numbness, dysarthria — that persists. It does not cause intermittent seconds-long shocks with a normal examination between them |\n| **Myofascial spasm** | a dull, aching, pressure-like pain from muscle, often related to jaw clenching or dental problems, **continuous or prolonged**, not paroxysmal and not electric. *(Not covered in the cached decks — general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material)* |\n\n**⚠️ One point of caution on the stem's last clause.** It reports “tenderness over the right maxillary area.” Palpable tenderness is not a feature of TN, and in isolation would suggest maxillary sinusitis or a dental cause. Here it is best read as the **trigger zone** being touched during the examination — everything else in the vignette is textbook TN, and no other diagnosis explains the electric, seconds-long, touch-triggered paroxysms.\n\n**Bottom line:** electric shocks in V2/V3, seconds long, set off by light touch, with a normal neurological examination, is trigeminal neuralgia.\n\n*Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.*",
    "objective": "Trigeminal neuralgia gives paroxysmal electric pain in V2/V3 triggered by light touch, with no weakness, no numbness and a normal examination.",
