    "stem": "The duration of an attack of Trigeminal Neuralgia is about:",
    "options": [
      "One minute",
      "3 hours",
      "10 minutes",
      "24 hours"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explanation": "**One minute — the shortest option offered, and the only one in the right order of magnitude. A trigeminal neuralgia paroxysm lasts SECONDS.**\n\n`L8) Cranial nerves` is explicit: “**Duration: several seconds**, may occur repetitively. Some patients with longstanding TN may have continuous dull pain that is present between paroxysms of pain.”\n\n**⚠️ The exactly correct answer is not on the menu, and that is worth knowing before the exam.** The lecture says *several seconds*; the shortest option printed is *one minute*. Answer **A** because it is the only choice within a plausible range of the truth — but carry **“seconds”** as the fact, not “one minute.” The bank prints no explanation for this question, so this is a judgement about which offered option is closest, not a disagreement with a printed key.\n\n**Why the duration is the single most useful discriminator in facial pain.** Every important cause of facial pain has a characteristic timescale, and the timescale alone narrows the diagnosis before any investigation:\n\n| Condition | Duration of one attack |\n|---|---|\n| **Trigeminal neuralgia** | **seconds** — up to about two minutes at the outside |\n| **Cluster headache** | **15–180 minutes**, in bouts, often waking the patient at the same hour |\n| **Migraine** | **4–72 hours** |\n| **Temporal arteritis, dental and sinus pain** | **constant**, over days |\n\n*(The comparative figures for cluster headache, migraine and arteritis are not stated in `L8` — general medical knowledge, not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**The word `L8` uses for the pattern is “paroxysms”**, and paroxysm is doing real work: the attacks are sudden in onset, sudden in offset, and **stereotyped** — the same pain, in the same place, every time. The deck's own worked example makes the frequency clear: “A 64 years old female presenting with acute attacks of pain in the Lt side of the face, **electric like of few seconds duration, attacks are very frequent 10–30 attacks/day**.” So the *total* burden can dominate a patient's day even though each individual attack is over in a moment.\n\n**One caveat the deck adds and examiners like:** “Some patients with **longstanding** TN may have **continuous dull pain** present **between** paroxysms.” That background ache does not contradict the diagnosis — but the diagnostic feature remains the seconds-long shocks superimposed on it.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong:** **3 hours** is the upper end of a **cluster headache** attack; **10 minutes** is far too long for a single paroxysm, though it may be how long a *run* of attacks lasts; **24 hours** is a **migraine** timescale and excludes TN outright.\n\n**Bottom line:** seconds, repeated many times a day. Anything measured in hours is not trigeminal neuralgia.\n\n*Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.*",
    "objective": "A trigeminal neuralgia paroxysm lasts seconds and may recur 10–30 times a day; attacks measured in hours point elsewhere.",
    "source": "neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.17 (answer p.20)"
