    "id": "npqb-nr-69",
    "bank": "gradegain",
    "module": "neuropsych",
    "chapter": "nr-cranial",
    "stem": "A 29-year-old female presents with drooping of the left side of her face and an inability to close her left eye. She had a viral illness in the preceding week. There is no past medical history. On examination, there is a left facial nerve palsy. The remaining cranial nerves are normal. Power, tone and reflexes are normal in the limbs. What is the best course of treatment?",
    "options": [
      "Intravenous immunoglobulin",
      "Oral Augmentin",
      "Oral steroids and oral acyclovir",
      "Oral valacyclovir",
      "Plasmapheresis"
    ],
    "answer": 2,
    "explanation": "**Oral steroids plus oral acyclovir. This is post-viral Bell's palsy, and the treatment is a short course of both — with eye care running alongside.**\n\n**The bank's printed explanation, verbatim:**\n\n> This is the classical history of a post-viral Bell's palsy. There is some evidence to support a short course of steroids and acyclovir. You need to be confident that; there are no features of Guillain-Barre (test reflexes), or brainstem vascular disease or space occupying lesion. The neurological examination of the cranial nerves thus needs to be completed with care.\n\n*Expanded here:*\n\n**`L8) Cranial nerves` supports both drugs and adds the doses:**\n\n> “**Prednisone. Only given within the first 5 days** at a dose of **1 mg/kg prednisone per day (up to 60 mg) for 7 days then gradual withdrawal.** **Acyclovir**; may be of value in the early stages of Bell palsy.”\n\n**⚠️ The five-day window is the single most important number here.** Steroids given late do not help. That makes Bell's palsy a condition where the *speed* of presentation changes the outcome, and it is why a facial droop is seen urgently rather than in a routine clinic.\n\n**Why acyclovir at all** — `L8`: “**The main etiologic agent is a herpes simplex virus-1 infection**”, and this patient had “a viral illness in the preceding week.” The same deck notes “approximately **60 %** of patients have a preceding viral illness.” The antiviral is adjunctive; **steroids are the part with the strongest evidence**.\n\n**⚠️ What the bank's answer leaves out, and what `L8` puts FIRST.** The deck opens its management slide with: “**Eye care. This is the primary concern in Bell palsy.** Lubricating eye drops during the day and an ointment-like petrolatum at night are usually effective.” This patient **cannot close her left eye**, so her cornea is exposed. `L8` lists “**exposure corneal ulcer**” as the **most dangerous** early complication. Drugs treat the nerve; eye care prevents the blindness. Question 80 of this topic tests precisely that. The deck also adds “**physiotherapy is very important to keep facial muscles function.**”\n\n**Why the examination findings in the stem matter.** The bank's box tells you to exclude three things, and the stem excludes each in turn:\n\n| Excluded by | How |\n|---|---|\n| **Guillain–Barré** | “power, tone and **reflexes are normal in the limbs**” — GBS is areflexic and ascending, and it causes **bilateral** facial weakness |\n| **Brainstem vascular disease** | “the **remaining cranial nerves are normal**” and there is no crossed hemiplegia — a pontine lesion would give both |\n| **Space-occupying lesion / CPA tumour** | no hearing loss; `L8` says a cerebello-pontine angle lesion gives “7th + 8th → **deafness (perceptive)**” |\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n- **IV immunoglobulin** and **plasmapheresis** are the treatments for **Guillain–Barré**, excluded by the normal reflexes and the unilateral, isolated palsy.\n- **Oral Augmentin** treats bacterial infection. There is no otitis media, no mastoiditis and no fever here.\n- **Oral valacyclovir** — an antiviral alone. Not wrong in principle, but **incomplete**: it omits the steroid, which is the component that improves recovery.\n\n**Prognosis, which patients always ask:** `L8` — “**Full recovery occurs in most of the patients. Recovery usually occurs in 4 months to 6 months.**”\n\n**Bottom line:** steroids within five days, acyclovir alongside, and protect the eye from the first consultation.",
    "objective": "Post-viral Bell's palsy is treated with oral steroids (1 mg/kg within 5 days) plus acyclovir, with eye care as the primary concern.",
